{
  "phenomenon": "Rayleigh scattering",
  "mechanism": "Shorter wavelengths (blue/violet) scatter more strongly off atmospheric molecules than longer wavelengths (red/orange). Sunlight enters the atmosphere; blue light redirects in all directions, reaching the observer from every part of the sky.",
  "key_factors": ["Wavelength dependence (scattering ∝ 1/λ⁴)", "Molecular size ≪ wavelength of visible light", "Sun angle (longer path at sunset shifts color to red)"],
  "common_misconception": "The sky is blue because it reflects the ocean. In reality, the ocean appears blue partly because it reflects the sky, and partly due to water's own absorption spectrum."
}
